{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Notebook 1 — Data Exploration\n",
    "\n",
    "**Goal:** Understand what the raw CTA data looks like *before* touching any models.\n",
    "\n",
    "This is the most important step in any statistical project. A model you build without understanding your data will give you answers you can't trust.\n",
    "\n",
    "We're going to answer:\n",
    "- What columns do we have, and what do they mean?\n",
    "- How many records are there? What time range do they cover?\n",
    "- What does the distribution of delays look like?\n",
    "- Are there obvious patterns by time-of-day or day-of-week?\n",
    "- Are there data quality issues we need to handle?\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "**Setup:** Make sure you have run the collector for at least a few hours before running this notebook."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import sys\n",
    "sys.path.insert(0, '..')  # let Python find the backend/ package\n",
    "\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import matplotlib.ticker as ticker\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "from sqlalchemy import text\n",
    "\n",
    "from backend.db.session import SessionLocal\n",
    "\n",
    "sns.set_theme(style='darkgrid', palette='muted')\n",
    "pd.set_option('display.max_columns', None)\n",
    "pd.set_option('display.float_format', '{:.2f}'.format)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Loading raw snapshots\n",
    "\n",
    "The `arrival_snapshots` table is our primary raw data source. Every ~25 seconds the collector calls the CTA Train Tracker API for each Red/Blue station and records every prediction it gets back.\n",
    "\n",
    "Key columns:\n",
    "| Column | What it means |\n",
    "|--------|---------------|\n",
    "| `snapshot_time` | When *we* called the API (UTC) |\n",
    "| `prdt` | When CTA computed the prediction |\n",
    "| `arr_t` | CTA's predicted arrival time at this station |\n",
    "| `run_number` | Unique train run identifier |\n",
    "| `station_id` | CTA parent station ID (mapid) |\n",
    "| `is_delayed` | CTA's own "delayed" flag |\n",
    "| `is_scheduled` | 1 = no live GPS, falling back to schedule |"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "with SessionLocal() as db:\n",
    "    snaps = pd.read_sql(\n",
    "        text(\"SELECT * FROM arrival_snapshots ORDER BY snapshot_time DESC LIMIT 200000\"),\n",
    "        db.bind,\n",
    "        parse_dates=['snapshot_time', 'prdt', 'arr_t']\n",
    "    )\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f'Rows loaded: {len(snaps):,}')\n",
    "print(f'Time range: {snaps.snapshot_time.min()} → {snaps.snapshot_time.max()}')\n",
    "snaps.head(3)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Basic shape and dtypes\n",
    "snaps.info()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Coverage check\n",
    "\n",
    "Before doing anything else, verify we actually have data for both lines across multiple days. Gaps here mean gaps in training data later."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Records by route\n",
    "print('Records by route:')\n",
    "print(snaps['route'].value_counts())\n",
    "\n",
    "# Records per day\n",
    "snaps['date'] = snaps['snapshot_time'].dt.date\n",
    "daily = snaps.groupby(['date', 'route']).size().unstack(fill_value=0)\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 3))\n",
    "daily.plot(ax=ax, color=['#c60c30', '#00a1de'])\n",
    "ax.set_title('Snapshot records per day by route')\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel('records')\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Computing raw delay from snapshots\n",
    "\n",
    "**Key insight:** `arr_t - snapshot_time` tells us how many minutes until this train was predicted to arrive *when we polled*. But this isn't the same as the delay.\n",
    "\n",
    "The **delay** is: `actual_arrival_time - scheduled_arrival_time`.\n",
    "\n",
    "We don't directly observe actual arrivals — we have to *infer* them from sequences of snapshots (see Notebook 2). For now, let's look at a proxy:\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Proxy delay** = `arr_t - prdt`  \n",
    "> This is how many minutes CTA predicted the train would take from *its own prediction timestamp*. If this is consistently more than the nominal headway, the train is running behind.\n",
    "\n",
    "This is the 'minutes_until_arrival' feature the CTA docs recommend computing."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "snaps['minutes_until'] = (\n",
    "    (snaps['arr_t'] - snaps['snapshot_time']).dt.total_seconds() / 60\n",
    ").clip(0, 60)  # cap outliers at 60 min for plotting\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(12, 4))\n",
    "\n",
    "for ax, (route, colour) in zip(axes, [('Red', '#c60c30'), ('Blue', '#00a1de')]):\n",
    "    subset = snaps[snaps['route'] == route]['minutes_until'].dropna()\n",
    "    ax.hist(subset, bins=60, color=colour, alpha=0.8, edgecolor='none')\n",
    "    ax.set_title(f'{route} Line — minutes until arrival distribution')\n",
    "    ax.set_xlabel('minutes')\n",
    "    ax.set_ylabel('snapshot count')\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "print('\\nSummary stats (minutes until arrival):')\n",
    "print(snaps.groupby('route')['minutes_until'].describe())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Time-of-day patterns\n",
    "\n",
    "One of the most powerful predictors of train lateness is *when* you're travelling. Rush hours have more trains, more crowding, more dwell time. Late nights have fewer trains and wider headways.\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's look at the volume of \"delayed\" flags by hour."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import pytz\n",
    "chicago = pytz.timezone('America/Chicago')\n",
    "\n",
    "snaps['local_time'] = snaps['snapshot_time'].dt.tz_convert(chicago)\n",
    "snaps['hour'] = snaps['local_time'].dt.hour\n",
    "snaps['dow']  = snaps['local_time'].dt.day_name()\n",
    "\n",
    "delay_by_hour = snaps.groupby(['route', 'hour'])['is_delayed'].mean().reset_index()\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 4))\n",
    "for route, colour in [('Red', '#c60c30'), ('Blue', '#00a1de')]:\n",
    "    d = delay_by_hour[delay_by_hour['route'] == route]\n",
    "    ax.plot(d['hour'], d['is_delayed'] * 100, marker='o', color=colour, label=route)\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_title('% of snapshots with CTA delayed flag — by hour (Chicago time)')\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel('Hour of day')\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel('% delayed')\n",
    "ax.set_xticks(range(0, 24))\n",
    "ax.legend()\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Data quality: missing values and flags\n",
    "\n",
    "Real-world data is messy. Before we build models we need to know:\n",
    "- How often is `arr_t` missing? (can't compute delay without it)\n",
    "- How often is `is_scheduled=True`? (schedule-based predictions are less reliable)\n",
    "- Are there anomalous run numbers or station IDs?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print('Null counts:')\n",
    "print(snaps[['arr_t', 'prdt', 'run_number', 'station_id']].isnull().sum())\n",
    "\n",
    "print('\\nFlag distribution:')\n",
    "print(snaps[['is_delayed', 'is_scheduled', 'is_faulty']].mean().rename('fraction True'))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# How many unique run numbers per day? (sanity check on collector coverage)\n",
    "snaps.groupby('date')['run_number'].nunique().plot(\n",
    "    kind='bar', figsize=(12, 3), title='Unique run numbers per day'\n",
    ")\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. Actual arrivals (if ETL has run)\n",
    "\n",
    "If you've run `python -m backend.etl.arrival_inference`, the `actual_arrivals` table will be populated. Let's check it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "with SessionLocal() as db:\n",
    "    arrivals = pd.read_sql(\n",
    "        text(\"SELECT * FROM actual_arrivals WHERE delay_minutes IS NOT NULL\"),\n",
    "        db.bind,\n",
    "        parse_dates=['actual_arrival_time', 'scheduled_arrival_time']\n",
    "    )\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f'Actual arrivals with matched schedule: {len(arrivals):,}')\n",
    "if len(arrivals) == 0:\n",
    "    print('Run `python -m backend.etl.arrival_inference` first.')\n",
    "else:\n",
    "    print(arrivals['delay_minutes'].describe())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "if len(arrivals) > 0:\n",
    "    # Clip extreme outliers for readability\n",
    "    delays = arrivals['delay_minutes'].clip(-10, 30)\n",
    "\n",
    "    fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10, 4))\n",
    "    ax.hist(delays, bins=80, color='steelblue', edgecolor='none', alpha=0.85)\n",
    "    ax.axvline(0, color='white', linestyle='--', linewidth=1, label='On time')\n",
    "    ax.axvline(2, color='orange', linestyle='--', linewidth=1, label='+2 min threshold')\n",
    "    ax.axvline(-1, color='skyblue', linestyle='--', linewidth=1, label='-1 min threshold')\n",
    "    ax.set_title('Distribution of actual delay (minutes)')\n",
    "    ax.set_xlabel('Delay (minutes) — negative = early, positive = late')\n",
    "    ax.legend()\n",
    "    plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "    plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Class balance\n",
    "    def label(d):\n",
    "        if d < -1: return 'ahead'\n",
    "        if d <= 2: return 'on_time'\n",
    "        return 'behind'\n",
    "\n",
    "    arrivals['status'] = arrivals['delay_minutes'].apply(label)\n",
    "    print('\\nClass balance:')\n",
    "    print(arrivals['status'].value_counts(normalize=True).mul(100).round(1))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Key takeaways from exploration\n",
    "\n",
    "Before moving on, write down answers to these questions based on your data:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. How many days of data do you have? (More = better models)\n",
    "2. What fraction of snapshots have `is_scheduled=True`? These are lower-quality predictions.\n",
    "3. Is the delay distribution **symmetric** around zero, or skewed toward late? (Skewed → median regression or log-transform might help)\n",
    "4. Is the class balance highly unequal? (e.g. 80% on_time → a \"predict always on_time\" classifier gets 80% accuracy for free — not impressive)\n",
    "5. Are there obvious peaks in the delayed-flag chart? Rush hours should show up.\n",
    "\n",
    "➡️ **Next:** [02_feature_engineering.ipynb](02_feature_engineering.ipynb)"
   ]
  }
 ],
